clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/aq-codex.md:5:The stored third-reader prompt explicitly assigns `merge_acceptable: false` to overlaps as well as wrong merges. I treated that field as a rejection under the acceptance line without checking the prompt’s definition. The correct reported result is **1/57 clear wrong**, with the **two overlaps** shown separately. The dev overlaps should likewise remain visible.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.md:3:For example, one grouped record combines AWS growth with separate Microsoft and Google Cloud growth. The third reader said it was not an acceptable repeat of the AWS-only record, but the acceptance count excludes it as “overlap.”
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.md:9:- **Wrong merges: the reported pass is not supported by the stored verdicts.** The third reader marked X7, X22 and X49 `merge_acceptable: false`. Counting all three gives **3/57 = 5.3%**, above 2%; the reported 1/57 counts only the verdict labeled “wrong.” X7 combines three companies’ results; X49 adds a separate 100-million-user goal to an 18-million-user figure. X49 is debatable because the shared figure is present, but it cannot be silently counted as acceptable after the reader marked it false. The dev draws have the same issue: their third readers marked 15, 13 and 16 disputed merges unacceptable, including overlaps, while Claude reports one wrong per draw. [Fresh verdicts]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/clean-acceptance-2026-11-09/step2/merge-third-answer.json)
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-codex.md:46:Preserve each reader’s actual verdict when counting; labels such as “overlap” cannot erase an explicit rejection. Count missing items in the complete-system view as well as the search-only view. Test whether grouped information is usable, not merely stored. Finally, distinguish a link that predicts an exact outcome from information that helps an analyst understand its risks.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/aa-codex.md:16:**(d) Order.** First complete the full prompt audit, source-to-answer census and no-cost contract check; register positive and negative controls, failure handling and thresholds. Then use the motivating cases, followed only if they pass by the 16×3 comparison. A dev forward run follows only after those checks pass; read misses and resulting library against sources. Use the exposed test set once on the final configuration. Preserve the planned source-backed review of Step 2’s three wrong merges.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/at-challenge.md:10:   - word or theme overlap ("the only thesis naming Rubin"; "same month, same AI-infrastructure financing theme");
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/at-codex.md:5:- **Mechanism:** Topic matching and speculative inference are supported as observed behaviors. But “coverage” is not the whole cause: c8 already says to match the question rather than word overlap and to link only what informs it. The model sometimes violated those instructions. Its later explanation is useful evidence, not proof that a closing check will correct the original choice.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/af-codex.md:18:- **Mapping and scoring:** [map_reference:43](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/map_reference.py:43) chooses passage overlap, which can map several old records to one new record. Audit collisions and unmapped pairs. [score_final:64](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/score_final.py:64) divides wrong merges by *all* merges even if disputes remain unjudged. Require complete judgments before a verdict. [tally_questions:16](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/tally_questions.py:16) uses `zip`, silently omitting missing answers; require exact IDs and counts.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/af-codex.md:30:Apply the agreed lines: helpful coverage **at least 81.2%**—the 190/234 dev line, rounded up to whole matters; search **at least 90%** of independently identified incoming repeats **and revisions**; clearly wrong merges **at most 2% of all merges**, with none unjudged; grouped dossiers retain **at least as many** later details as ungrouped dossiers. Report must-open coverage and must-not openings; D384 replaced the earlier small-thesis line, but no numerical threshold for fresh-reader judgments has been agreed. Set it through council and Alex before selection.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/v-claude.md:39:6. **Possible cross-step link (hypothesis):** 15 of Step 2's overlaps and 2 of its 3 wrong merges come from bundled Step 1 records,
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/v-claude.md:52:3. Step 2 wrong merges: bundles and one Jev figure error.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/w-codex.md:1:I agree: **audit and test the smallest conditions change first**, then investigate the wrong merges. Your new evidence also corrects my Step 2 emphasis: the 600-character cut did **not** cause the three recorded wrong merges, although it remains a separate risk.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ao-claude.md:32:| Wrong merges | 1/57 (1.8%) | 1/100, 1/100, 1/106 (1.0 / 1.0 / 0.9%); every dispute judged by a third reader |
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ag-challenge.md:19:- Readers: two independent Codex readers per article list the article's matters from the source first, then judge kept items (helpful / not helpful / neutral), missed helpful matters, each opened thesis against the standard's four reasons and tests (big matter or not; must-not kinds), links, and each merge (acceptable / overlap / wrong, third reader on disputes). Disagreements resolved against the source by a third reader.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ag-challenge.md:20:- Pass lines: helpful coverage at least 81.2% of reader-identified helpful matters; wrong merges at most 2% with none unjudged; search at least 90% of reader-identified repeats; grouped detail retention not below ungrouped on two predeclared dossiers; thesis line: proposed "theses that fail the standard's opening tests at most 1 per 25 articles", to go to Alex for his number.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/v-codex.md:5:**Step 2.** The isolated comparison and grouping path ran on 282 Step 1 records and produced 178 groups. In one fresh comparison draw, search found a group containing a known counterpart in **112/113** cases; readers found **3 clearly wrong merges out of 104**, above Alex’s 2% limit; and the grouped view retained **28/36** later details versus **27.5/36** without merging. Thus the recorded search and detail checks met their lines, while wrong merges failed. The search denominator contains known labelled counterparts, not all real repeats. The tested comparison also truncates passages at 600 characters; 44/282 stored items exceed that length, so unseen qualifications may affect comparison. [Step 2 results](clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md), [comparison code](clean-arch/step2/compare.py), [conditions design §15](clean-arch/STEP1-CONDITIONS-DESIGN.md).
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/v-codex.md:15:4. **Merging sources alone erased later detail.** Keeping each report’s own text in a group restored detail in the selected reader exercise. But grouping does not make a wrong association right: two of the three clear wrong merges involved a bundled original, and one involved different analyst figures. [Step 2 log §§10–12](clean-arch/step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md).
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/v-codex.md:32:The idea is falsified as a usable fix if it cannot simultaneously retain the useful unfiled figure, discard the ruled-out cash example, preserve the margin link, and avoid figure-only views across the repeated cases. A successful small check would justify the broader forward measurement; it would not by itself establish acceptance. Step 2’s wrong merges remain open and should be addressed within the same Steps 1–2 boundary.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/w-challenge.md:18:- **Conclusion:** the cut explains 0 of the 3 wrong merges. Fixing it is a latent-defect fix, not the line-2 fix.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/w-challenge.md:43:3. **Step 2 line 2.** Two of the 3 wrong merges come from one bundled Step 1 record.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ap-handoff.md:8:- the dev draws in `poc/data/clean-step1-c8-dev-d{1,2,3}-2026-11-09/` (`step2/merge-third-answer.json`, `step2/merge-key.json`), and
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/ee-claude.md:14:- clear wrong merges 1/57;
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/av-codex.md:17:The decisive improvement was a product decision: useful material could survive without an existing question. That removed the need to invent a question merely to save a fact. Separating a report from another party’s denial also addressed a grouping cascade in which one bundled record caused six wrong merges.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/av-codex.md:23:- **“The cause is found”** overstates the evidence. The model reported topic overlap and inference chains, but no intervention has isolated or fixed their contribution.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/x-codex.md:8:- **Existing/new links, all relevant views, narrowest, periods and examples** (system 47–54, 63–84; closing 13): **consistent** with D371 and standard §4’s separation test. The blanket instruction to link *every* informed view alongside “narrowest” is **abstract** about overlapping views.
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/aq-challenge.md:3:1. **Wrong merges.** I disagree with 3/57, for this reason. The line Alex chose is "clear wrong merges at most 2%" (D381,
clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/council/aq-challenge.md:4:   `docs/DECISIONS-log.md` line 4103). The Step 2 close-out measured it by the third reader's `wrong` verdict and reported overlaps
