avy North America segment saw revenue increase 16% year over year, and total company revenue was up 20% to $200.6 bil", "and it's not as if the company hasn't been posting strong results. The tech giant's Amazon Web Services (AWS) cloud infrastructure segment grew sales 37% year over year to reach $42.2 billion in the second quarter, with AI-related demand helping the unit record its strongest growth rate since 2021. For reference, the average analyst estimate had called for annual growth of roughly 31% and sales of $40.54 billion in the quarter.\nMeanwhile, revenue for the company's e-commerce-heavy North America segment saw revenue increase 16% year over year, and total company revenue was up 20% to $200.6 billion in the quarter. Amazon's sales growth is accelerating again, and there are good reasons to think that the business is still in "], "theses": []}, "later": {"summary": "Microsoft's Azure revenue grew 43% year over year in its fiscal 2026 fourth quarter (ended June 30, 2026), Amazon Web Services net sales rose 37% year over year in the second quarter, and Alphabet's Google Cloud revenue rose 82% year over year in the second quarter, which fool.com cites as early signs that AI infrastructure spending is paying off for cloud customers.", "original_party": "Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet (reported results)", "statement_date": null, "period": "Microsoft fiscal Q4 2026 (quarter ended June 30, 2026); Amazon and Alphabet second quarter (calendar Q2 2026)", "publication_date": "2026-09-05", "tier": "fact", "reporting_speaker": "Manali Pradhan, CFA (fool.com)", "publisher": "fool.com", "title": "Most Investors Overpay for AI Hype. Nvidia Is the Exception.", "passages": ["There are encouraging signs. Microsoft 's Azure revenue grew 43% year over year in the fourth quarter of its fiscal 2026 (which ended June 30), while Amazon Web Services' net sales increased 37% year over year in the second quarter. Alphabet 's Google Cloud revenue also surged 82% year over year in the second quarter, driven by strong demand for AI infrastructure and AI solutions."], "source_context": ["he company's risks. Nvidia has warned that shortages of land, power, and other data center infrastructure could delay customer deployments of its hardware.\nNvidia's growth will also depend heavily on whether its customers can earn attractive returns from their massive AI infrastructure investments. There are encouraging signs. Microsoft 's Azure revenue grew 43% year over year in the fourth quarter of its fiscal 2026 (which ended June 30), while Amazon Web Services' net sales increased 37% year over year in the second quarter. Alphabet 's Google Cloud revenue also surged 82% year over year in the second quarter, driven by strong demand for AI infrastructure and AI solutions.\nWhile these growth rates suggest that AI infrastructure spending is already helping cloud companies, they do not yet prove that the huge amounts being invested in new AI capacity will generate sufficiently high returns over time to justify the outlays.\nNvidia is also making increasingly large finan"], "theses": ["Will the combined capital expenditures of the top five hyperscalers approach $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027, as Nvidia expects?"]}}
{"id": "X22", "earlier": {"summary": "Nvidia said its Vera Rubin system is already in production, and management expects the new platform to account for about 20% of data center segment revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.", "original_party": "Nvidia", "statement_date": null, "period": "Fiscal Q3 2027 (the quarter management guides for the ~20% share); production status as of the fiscal Q2 2027 report", "publication_date": "2026-09-05", "tier": "party_statement", "reporting_speaker": "Manali Pradhan, CFA (fool.com)", "publisher": "fool.com", "title": "Most Investors Overpay for AI Hype. Nvidia Is the Exception.", "passages": ["Nvidia's big upcoming growth driver is the Vera Rubin system, which is already in production. Management expects the new platform to account for about 20% of data center segment revenue in the third quarter."], "source_context": ["s of $9.26 per share. But the multiple falls to around 15 times based on the analysts' fiscal 2028 earnings estimate of $15.59 per share. Hence, I believe that the attractiveness of Nvidia's valuation depends significantly on whether the company can deliver the growth Wall Street currently expects.\nNvidia's big upcoming growth driver is the Vera Rubin system, which is already in production. Management expects the new platform to account for about 20% of data center segment revenue in the third quarter. Nvidia has also received purchase orders for Vera Rubin from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud provider, and equipment manufacturer.\nNvidia estimates that its revenue opportunity for every gigawatt (a measure of power capacity) of AI infrastructure has increased from around $18 billion with its Hop"], "theses": ["Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, the next-generation system now in production and receiving purchase orders from hyperscalers, account for about 20% of the data center segment's revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, as management guided?"]}, "later": {"summary": "Nvidia said on its fiscal Q2 2027 analyst call that systems based on its Vera Rubin architecture had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026.", "original_party": "Nvidia", "statement_date": "August 2026", "period": "August 2026", "publication_date": "2026-09-06", "tier": "party_statement", "reporting_speaker": "calcalistech.com", "publisher": "calcalistech.com", "title": "Nvidia is no longer just an AI chip company. It is financing the revolution it built", "passages": ["During the analyst call following the earnings release, Nvidia said that systems based on Vera Rubin had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026."], "source_context": ["ocessors, AI chips, memory and communications, allowing large systems to operate faster and more efficiently. Vera Rubin is designed not only for training models but also for optimizing inference, producing more responses in less time and with lower power consumption, particularly for large models. During the analyst call following the earnings release, Nvidia said that systems based on Vera Rubin had already been delivered commercially to customers during August 2026.\nOver the weekend, Nvidia announced that it had agreed to acquire Hugging Face for $11.9 billion in cash and another $1 billion in options. Hugging Face will bring Nvidia models, a developer community and runtime capabilities. Together with the Groq deal, the acquisitions expand Nvidia’s product and"], "theses": ["Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform, the next-generation system now in production and receiving purchase orders from hyperscalers, account for about 20% of the data center segment's revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, as management guided?"]}}

